== Problem

The problem is the same as in the previous labs: select 50% of the nodes to form a Hamiltonian cycle, minimizing the sum of edge and node costs. Distances are Euclidean and rounded half-up, so all values are integers stored in floating point.

== Local search with a move evaluations cache (LM)

Steepest local search re-evaluates the whole neighbourhood after every accepted move, even though most of the previous deltas are still valid or only slightly stale. We speed it up by storing every improving move ($Delta < 0$) in a sorted list `LM` and re-using the stored deltas instead of recomputing them. The neighbourhood is the one that performed best in Lab #4: *intra-route two-edge exchange (2-opt)* and *inter-route exchange of a selected node with an unselected node*.

The algorithm follows the "alternative" (batch refill) variant: after an accepted move there is *no* incremental repair of `LM`; we simply rescan it, and only when a full pass finds no applicable move do we rebuild the list with one complete evaluation of the neighbourhood on the current solution.

=== Main loop

#pseudocode-list(booktabs: true, title: [Steepest local search with LM])[
+ *Input:* tour $x$, distance matrix $D$, node costs $C$
+ *Output:* local optimum $x$
+ $"LM" :=$ *full-evaluation*$(x)$
+ *while* $"LM"$ is not empty *do*
  + $"applied" :=$ *false*
  + *for* every move $m$ in $"LM"$ (best $Delta$ first) *do*
    + *if* $m$ is broken *then*
      + remove $m$ from $"LM"$
    + *else if* $m$ is temporarily inapplicable (inverted relative edge direction) *then*
      + skip $m$, keep it in $"LM"$
    + *else*
      + apply $m$ to $x$, remove $m$ from $"LM"$
      + $"applied" :=$ *true*
      + *break* _\// restart the scan from the top of LM_
  + *if* not $"applied"$ *then*
    + $"LM" :=$ *full-evaluation*$(x)$   _\// batch refill_
    + *if* $"LM"$ is empty *then* *return* $x$  _\// local optimum_
+ *return* $x$
]

=== Full evaluation (initial fill and refill)

#pseudocode-list(booktabs: true, title: [Full neighbourhood evaluation])[
+ *for* every pair of non-adjacent edges $(a,b)$, $(c,d)$ in the same cycle *do*
  + $Delta_1 := D(a,c) + D(b,d) - D(a,b) - D(c,d)$   _\// standard 2-opt_
  + *if* $Delta_1 < 0$ *then* add move $(a,b),(c,d)$ to $"LM"$
  + $Delta_2 := D(a,d) + D(b,c) - D(a,b) - D(c,d)$   _\// inverted-edge 2-opt_
  + *if* $Delta_2 < 0$ *then* add move $(a,b),(d,c)$ to $"LM"$
    _\// stored with the second edge reversed: it becomes valid once a later_
    _\// 2-opt move flips one of the two edges_
+ *for* every selected node $u$ and every unselected node $v$ *do*
  + $Delta := D(u_"prev",v) + D(v,u_"next") - D(u_"prev",u) - D(u,u_"next") + C(v) - C(u)$
  + *if* $Delta < 0$ *then* add move $(u,v)$ with saved neighbours $(u_"prev",u_"next")$ to $"LM"$
+ sort $"LM"$ by $Delta$, most negative first
]

=== Move applicability during the scan

#pseudocode-list(booktabs: true, title: [Applicability of a cached 2-opt move])[
+ _\// $e_1 = (a,b)$, $e_2 = (c,d)$ as stored in the move_
+ *if* $e_1$ does not exist in either direction *or* $e_2$ does not exist *then*
  + broken $->$ remove from $"LM"$
+ *else if* one edge is stored forward and the other is stored backward *then*
  + temporarily inapplicable $->$ skip, keep in $"LM"$
+ *else*   _\// both forward or both backward_
  + apply 2-opt between the two edges, recompute `next`/`prev`/`route` arrays
]

For an inter-route move the check is: both nodes still exist, $u$ is still selected, $v$ is still unselected, and the current neighbours of $u$ are still the saved pair ${u_"prev", u_"next"}$ in either order (a 2-opt move may have reversed that part of the cycle). If any condition fails, the move is broken and is removed.

=== Implementation notes

To keep the constant factor low we maintain flat arrays `next_node`, `prev_node` and `route` next to the cycle vector, so an edge orientation test is $O(1)$. `LM` is a single `std::vector` whose capacity is reused (`clear()` instead of reallocation); removal from the middle of the list uses a `bool active` flag, so a scan never shifts elements. All 200 runs use the same $200$ random starting tours as the baseline steepest local search (the RNG is seeded from the start-node index), which makes the comparison paired.

