== Conclusions

*Quality.* Re-using cached deltas does not degrade the result: both variants converge to local optima of the same quality, and the average objective of LM is even slightly lower on both instances. This is expected, because the scan order inside `LM` differs from the canonical neighbourhood order, so the two searches do not always take the same path — the differences are within the noise observed earlier between steepest and greedy variants.

*Runtime.* The gain comes from three sources: (1) most iterations reuse stored deltas instead of recomputing ~15 thousand of them, (2) a full evaluation is performed only when `LM` runs dry, and (3) the flat `next`/`prev`/`route` arrays make the applicability test $O(1)$. The measured ~94% reduction is much larger than the reduction reported for candidate moves in Lab #4, which indicates that the dominant cost of full steepest local search really is the repeated evaluation of the whole neighbourhood rather than the tour update itself.

*Broken vs inverted moves.* Treating inverted-edge 2-opt moves as *temporarily inapplicable* instead of discarding them is what makes the list reusable: a 2-opt move reverses a segment, so moves that were inverted before an accepted move frequently become valid after it. Broken moves (edges that no longer exist) are removed immediately, which keeps `LM` short.

